{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 american biographical drama film directed by david fincher and written by the screenwriter jesse eisenberg. the film is based on the book \"the accidental billionaire\" by ben Mezrich, which tells the story of the founding of the social networking site facebook and the subsequent legal battles that followed.\n\nthe film stars jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, the co-founder and ceo of facebook, along with joshua jackson, andrew garfield, justin timberlake, and armie hammer. the film also features a supporting cast that includes patricia clarkson, max minghella, and ron leibman.\n\nthe film opens with a scene in which mark zuckerberg (played by jesse eisenberg) is seen as a social outcast in his sophomore year at harvard university. he is befriended by erin (played by karen gillan) and eventually meets eduardo saverin (played by armie hammer), a brazilian student who becomes his close friend and confidant. together, mark and eduardo come up with the idea of a social networking site that would allow harvard students to connect with each other.\n\nas the site gains popularity, mark and eduardo recruit other students, including harvard student casey (played by andrew garfield) and harvard student sean parker (played by justin timberlake), to help them expand the site. the film then explores the subsequent power struggles and conflicts that arise between mark and eduardo, as well as the legal battles that ensue when the site is sued by several students who claim that mark stole their idea.\n\none of the standout features of the film is its strong performances from the cast, particularly jesse eisenberg, who delivers a nuanced and compelling portrayal of mark zuckerberg. his performance is particularly impressive given the fact that the film is largely based on a true story and that eisenberg's portrayal of zuckerberg has been widely praised for its accuracy.\n\nthe film's direction by david fincher is also noteworthy, with the director bringing a level of sophistication and style to the film that is reminiscent of his earlier work on films such as \"se7en\" and \"fight club\". the film's cinematography is also noteworthy, with the director using a range of techniques to create a sense of tension and unease that perfectly captures the mood and atmosphere of the film.\n\nthe film's score by trent reznor and atticus ross is also noteworthy, with the pair delivering a haunting and atmospheric soundtrack that perfectly complements the film's mood and atmosphere. the score is particularly effective in capturing the sense of unease and tension that pervades much of the film, and is a key element in creating the film's overall sense of unease and foreboding.\n\nin terms of its themes, the film explores a range of issues, including the impact of technology on society, the nature of friendship and relationships, and the dangers of ambition and the pursuit of power. the film also raises questions about the ethics of business and the consequences of one's actions, particularly in the context of the legal battles that ensue when the site is sued.\n\noverall, the social network is a compelling and thought-provoking film that is well worth watching. its strong performances, sophisticated direction, and haunting score make it a must-see for fans of drama and biographical films. while the film may not be entirely accurate in its portrayal of the events that it depicts, it is a compelling and engaging film that is sure to leave viewers thinking long after the credits have rolled.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.045232724}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0016868184}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -7.6293654e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.18040486}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.00012385078}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.32515162}, {"token": "201", "logprob": -2.9086643e-05}, {"t